    "lawCode": "Add §13.04, Pks & Rec L; add §344-g, amd §§349-a & 342-ww, Hway L; amd §356, Pub Auth L; amd §§13-0103 & 13-0307, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6827\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the parks, recreation and historic\npreservation law, in relation to renaming Riverbank state park; to\nauthorize the renaming of the National Purple Heart Hall of Honor; to\nauthorize the renaming of, in whole or in part, the New Windsor\nCantonment state historic site; to amend the highway law, in relation\nto designating certain portions of the state highway system as the\n\"Senator William J. Larkin, Jr.  Highway\"; and to amend the public\nauthorities law, environmental conservation law and the highway law,\nin relation to naming the Governor Mario M. Cuomo Bridge\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThis legislation would rename Riverbank State Park and the National\nPurple Heart Hall of Honor and adjacent roads, and name the new bridge\nat Tappan Zee to honor the significant contributions of three\ndedicated public servants.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would add a new Section 13.04 to the Parks,\nRecreation and Historic Preservation law to rename the Riverbank State\nPark, located on the upper west side of Manhattan, as the \"Assemblyman\nHerman D. Farrell, Jr. State Park\".\n\nSections 2 -5 of the bill would add a new Section 344-g to the Highway\nLaw to rename a portion of state route 300 as the \"Senator William J.\nLarkin, Jr. Highway\". Further, the bill would allow the Commissioner\nof the Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation to take\nall steps necessary to obtain the authorization of the Palisades\nInterstate Park Commission to rename the National Purple Heart Hall of\nHonor and adjacent parkland in honor of Senator William J. Larkin, Jr.\n\nSections 6-10 of the bill would amend various provisions of the Public\nAuthorities Law, the Environmental Conservation Law and the Highway\nLaw to name the crossing over the Hudson River to the \"Governor Mario\nM. Cuomo Bridge\".\n\nSection 11 of the bill would make it effective immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis legislation would rename Riverbank State Park, and the National\nPurple Heart Hall of Honor and adjacent roads, and name the new bridge\nat Tappan Zee to honor the significant contributions of three\ndedicated public servants.\n\nFirst, this legislation would honor the distinguished career of\nAssemblyman Herman D. Farrell, Jr. Assemblyman Farrell's career of\nservice spans five decades in the New York State Assembly, culminating\nin his appointment as Chairman of the Ways and Means Committee. Among\nhis numerous legislative accomplishments, Assemblyman Farrell\nsponsored the Omnibus Consumer Protection and Banking Legislation Act,\nand his efforts led to the passage of legislation to provide greater\nand more accessible taxi service to his district, and the outer\nBurroughs as a whole.\n\nAssemblyman Farrell has also been a staunch advocate for his district.\nHis tireless efforts led to the state-funded construction of a\npedestrian bridge linking Hamilton Heights with Riverbank State Park\nand the Greenway, thus ensuring safe and efficient access to the\nGreenway and surrounding parks, including the Riverbank State Park,\nfor his constituents. It is only fitting that the Riverbank State Park\nbe renamed in his honor.\n\nSecond, this legislation would honor the distinguished career of\nSenator William J. Larkin, Jr. Senator Larkin spent the first 23 years\nof his career in the military and retired from the U.S. Army in 1967\nwith the rank of Lt. Colonel. In 1979, he was elected to the New York\nState Assembly. In 1990, Senator Larkin was elected serve the 39th\nSenate District where he currently sits today.\n\nSenator Larkin has worked tirelessly for his constituents, and has\nfought to ensure protections necessary for the State's servicemen and\nwomen. Among other accomplishments, he has sponsored significant\nreforms to veterans' benefit laws to increase financial protections\nfor families of U.S. servicemen fighting in the Persian Gulf War, and\nconflicts in Lebanon, Panama and Grenada. He was also instrumental to\nthe development and completion of the National Purple Heart Hall of\nHonor in New Windsor New York.\n\nTo honor his accomplishments and work on behalf of the State's\nveterans, the legislation would direct the Office of Parks, Recreation\nand Historic Preservation to work with the Palisades Interstate Park\nCommission to facilitate renaming the National Purple Heart Hall of\nHonor to the Senator William J. Larkin, Jr. National Purple Heart Hall\nof Honor, and for the renaming of all or a portion of the New Windsor\nCantonment State Historic Site in his honor. This bill would also\nauthorize the roads leading up to the Hall of Honor to be remained the\nSenator William J. Larkin, Jr. Highway.\n\nFinally, this legislation would honor the distinguished career of\nGovernor Mario M. Cuomo. Driven by his steadfast goal of leaving the\nworld a better place and ensuring justice for all, Governor Mario\nCuomo served as a moral beacon for the people of New York. As\nGovernor, Mario Cuomo served for three terms from 1983 until 1994.\nGovernor Cuomo also served in the capacities of Lt. Governor from 1979\nto 1982, and as Secretary of the State of New York from 1975 to 1978\nunder Governor Hugh Carey.\n\nAs a young lawyer in Queens, Mario Cuomo entered public life by\nrepresenting working-class homeowners in Corona. The experience forged\nhis commitment to creative problem solving through compromise without\nlosing sight of his firm moral principle. During his tenure as\nGovernor, Mario Cuomo faced numerous challenges, including prison\nuprisings, the AIDS epidemic, and national economic recession. He was\nthe driving force behind sweeping comprehensive fiscal and ethics\nreforms, and oversaw the passage of the first seat belt law in New\nYork State.\n\nAdditionally, Governor Cuomo took significant action for the health\nand safety of New York's elderly by making prescription drugs more\naffordable for seniors and for New York's children by expanding access\nto health care coverage and educational opportunities. The Governor\nalso served as a moral beacon for the state and the country by vetoing\n12 bills over the course of his gubernatorial term that would have\nreinstated the death penalty. Finally, Governor Cuomo dedicated\nsignificant efforts to rebuilding New York's roads and bridges through\nthe establishment of the \"Rebuild NY\" Transportation Bond Act.\n\nIn commemoration of his tremendous service to the state, this bill\nwould name the new bridge at Tappan Zee the Governor Mario M. Cuomo\nBridge. The naming of this bridge will serve as a physical reminder of\nthe Governor's lasting and important legacy to New York.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNone.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe bill would take effect immediately.\n",
